      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14194746/assets/52d37029cc93ea1e0be1c9a1488aa3275437c4be2b749ff09a497935de8399a4.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(x)$ is shown above. Approximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=4$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=2.$",
      "worked_solution": "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{g(x+h)-g(x-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat the point $x=4$ with step size $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(4) & ≈\\frac{g(4+2)-g(4-2)}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(6)-g(2)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
